{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DwjqXishFT8mSNYb7gq7qn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Examiner charge — e6-t08 (verify-independent)\n2\t\n3\tYou are the root-launched independent examiner for task e6-t08. You did not\n4\tauthor the provisional answer; your search and argument are what makes\n5\tevidence requirement E-B admissible. Re-derive everything below from\n6\t`package/artifacts/merge.py` and `package/task.json` yourself; do not treat\n7\tthe worker's conclusions as given.\n8\t\n9\t## Provisional answer under examination\n10\t\n11\t- Verdict: **VIOLATED**.\n12\t- Claimed violating input: `[[0, 1], [1, 2]]`.\n13\t- Claimed mechanism: line 8 of merge.py uses the strict comparison\n14\t  `s < out[-1][1]`, so an interval whose start equals the previous output\n15\t  interval's end is appended unmerged; the claim forbids `i.end >= j.start`\n16\t  in the sorted output, and equality then occurs.\n17\t\n18\t## Answer components whose failure would change the deliverable\n19\t\n20\tAttack each of these specifically; refuting any one flips or invalidates\n21\tthe verdict:\n22\t\n23\t1. **Claim reading.** Confirm the claim in package/task.json really uses\n24\t   `>=` (so touching output intervals count as violations). If the claim\n25\t   only forbade strict overlap (`>`), the verdict would be HOLDS — check\n26\t   whether any in-domain input can make merge emit a pair with\n27\t   `i.end > j.start` (the worker argues it cannot, because line 8 merges\n28\t   all strict overlaps).\n29\t2. **Domain membership.** Verify `[[0,1],[1,2]]` satisfies: at most 6\n30\t   intervals, integer endpoints in [-10, 10], start <= end for each.\n31\t3. **Mechanical trace.** Execute merge.py's logic by hand on\n32\t   `[[0,1],[1,2]]`: sorting, `out = [[0,1]]`, the test `1 < 1`, the append,\n33\t   and the returned value. Confirm the sorted output contains a pair\n34\t   i before j with `i.end >= j.start`. If the trace instead merges the\n35\t   intervals (i.e. if line 8 were actually `<=`), the demonstration fails\n36\t   and the verdict must be re-derived from scratch.\n37\t\n38\t## Required independent search (E-B)\n39\t\n40\tCarry out your own systematic search of the domain, not a replay of the\n41\tworker's example:\n42\t\n43\t- Enumerate the boundary classes structurally: touching pairs\n44\t  (`[a,b],[b,c]`), zero-width intervals (`[a,a]` alone, duplicated, and\n45\t  touching a wider interval), nested intervals, duplicates, proper\n46\t  overlaps, empty and singleton lists, negative endpoints, and unsorted\n47\t  presentations of each.\n48\t- For each class, decide from the code whether merge's output can contain a\n49\t  forbidden pair, and record at least one concrete in-domain witness for\n50\t  every class you find violating (do not reuse `[[0,1],[1,2]]` as your only\n51\t  witness; e.g. check a zero-width case and a negative-endpoint case too).\n52\t- If you conclude no violating input exists in the domain, you must instead\n53\t  produce the E-B argument for that: a case analysis over the loop\n54\t  invariant of merge.py showing every reachable output satisfies\n55\t  `i.end < j.start` for consecutive pairs — and then explain the\n56\t  discrepancy with the worker's trace explicitly.\n57\t\n58\t## Checking method and report\n59\t\n60\tWork read-only. Use hand execution / symbolic tracing of the actual code\n61\ttext (no idealized merge). Report, in your session's output: (a) PASS/FAIL\n62\tper numbered component above, (b) your independent search record by class\n63\twith witnesses, (c) your final independent verdict HOLDS/VIOLATED, and\n64\t(d) whether the deliverable in result.json should stand unchanged.\n65\tDeterministic root verification, not this charge, decides promotion.\n66\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8db6ed65-af74-4f6e-87e3-27fd3f74b3f1","uuid":"0379a4ba-f80d-4a7f-8326-fdc0b9c2cfb5","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:48:07.519Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","fil